WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. Today I'd like to share some good news with you because lately I've been feeling a little bit BLEH

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.240
You know I'm kind of fed up with 2017 in general

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.540
and I find large swaths of the social internet sort of disheartening, and I just feel BLEH

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.260
But once a year I like to look at some encouraging global trend lines,

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.360
so here are five ways that 2017 is actually kind of the best.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:26.020
First, charitable giving is at an all-time high, even after adjusting for inflation, at least in the United States.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:31.740
Secondly, it's possible that 2017 will be the second to last year that any human ever gets polio.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.300
The end of polio will be a huge global health victory won by millions of donors and heroic volunteers.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:43.460
So far this year there have been only 16 new cases of wild polio virus. That's down from 37 last year.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.200
For a little bit of context, when I was in high school there were over a hundred thousand cases of polio every year.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.560
Third, by some measures, 2017 has been the best year in human history,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.220
although by many other measures, it hasn't been, like if you measure, for instance, by Twitter.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:01.000
But like percentage of people living in absolute poverty - lowest it's ever been; percentage of kids in school - highest its ever been;

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.980
percentage of adults who can read - highest it's ever been; cancer death rates are down,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.240
not just in rich countries, but globally; and also this baby hippopotamus was born!

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.040
Of course, there are ways in which the world is getting worse. In the US for instance,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.920
opiate abuse is driving a huge increase in death by drug overdose.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.680
South Sudan is experiencing a famine, the world's first official famine since 2012, and several other countries are on the brink of famine.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.140
Horrific ethnic cleansing in Myanmar has killed many people and forced hundreds of thousands more from their homes.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.400
There are many tragedies that demand better responses from governments and individuals.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.880
But even so, on average, the human

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.620
population is healthier, more prosperous, and better educated than it ever has been.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:44.880
Okay, fourth: babies. Babies are amazing and they are being born everyday and in 2017, a particular amazing baby named

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:51.840
Orin learned how to crawl. Also, babies have never been as healthy or as likely to survive into adulthood as they are in 2017.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.840
To understand how dramatically things are improving let's look at Liberia. So back in 1995 when I graduated from high school

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.080
24% of all babies born in Liberia died before the age of five.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.480
Today, it's under seven percent. Now, that is still way too high,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.140
but it's dropped by more than two-thirds in just 22 years.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.740
Now, you might be saying, "Oh, but population growth is out of control!" except NO.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:19.700
Like everywhere else in the world, in Liberia when health care access improves, and child mortality goes down, families choose to have fewer children.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.800
Here's what the fertility rate looks like since 1995.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.680
So these changes are profound, but I should say that they are neither natural nor inevitable.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:34.580
Like, this is happening because of investments we are making as a species: in research, in health care systems, and in training and paying healthcare workers.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:42.220
For most of human history, there was no expectation that over time lives would get healthier or food more abundant, because that's not what happened over time.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.800
It can be hard to process just how dramatic these changes are,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.520
so for a little bit of context, in the year my mother was born the child mortality rate in Portugal was about 9%.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.160
In 2017, no country on earth will have a child mortality rate that high.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.260
Now, lots of organizations are working to keep this progress going, but I want to highlight two:

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.960
Save the Children, which helps kids and families from refugee camps to remote villages worldwide,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.120
and Last Mile Health, which trains and employs community health workers in Liberia.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.980
Which brings me to my fifth bit of good news: The Project for Awesome starts this Friday at noon Eastern!

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.500
That's when you should upload your video about a charity you care about, more info and thumbnails available at projectforawesome.com

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:23.660
But the fundraiser for the project for awesome begins at midnight tonight and during this first half of The Project for Awesome

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.600
we're raising money for Save the Children and Last Mile Health.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.000
Because of matching donations, every dollar you donate will count as four dollars,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.300
and also you can get amazing perks like tuatara coins, and deleted scenes from my book Turtles All the Way Down, and a personalized self-care bunny.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.220
And some of those perks are limited in quantity, so no pressure, em dash, a little bit of pressure.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.400
If it's past midnight you can find a link to the fundraiser at projectforawesome.com

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.440
or in the doobly-doo below.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:52.360
Hank, I will see you and the rest of nerdfighteria at noon Eastern Time this Friday for the 48 hour Project for Awesome livestream. DFTBA.

